        {
            "author": {
                "avatarUrl": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/128352186?u=6d41c4a4265d45f9a0ce2949374e78fbc851bc1e&v=4",
                "login": "YoungStovallbL",
                "resourcePath": "/YoungStovallbL",
                "url": "https://github.com/YoungStovallbL"
            },
            "authorAssociation": "NONE",
            "body": "Hey there!\r\n\r\nSo it looks like you're wondering if it's possible to use the mini.pairs function in Python to surround a docstring with six quotes instead of the usual three, is that right?\r\n\r\nUnfortunately, it sounds like that's not possible with mini.pairs. The function is designed to work with two-character pairs (like parentheses or curly braces) and can only be conditioned by a two-character neighborhood. It's really more suited for use with code snippets.\r\n\r\nHowever, the good news is that the mini.snippets module is planned for future development, which should provide expanded functionality for this type of thing.",
            "bodyHTML": "<p dir=\"auto\">Hey there!</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">So it looks like you're wondering if it's possible to use the mini.pairs function in Python to surround a docstring with six quotes instead of the usual three, is that right?</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">Unfortunately, it sounds like that's not possible with mini.pairs. The function is designed to work with two-character pairs (like parentheses or curly braces) and can only be conditioned by a two-character neighborhood. It's really more suited for use with code snippets.</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">However, the good news is that the mini.snippets module is planned for future development, which should provide expanded functionality for this type of thing.</p>",
            "bodyText": "Hey there!\nSo it looks like you're wondering if it's possible to use the mini.pairs function in Python to surround a docstring with six quotes instead of the usual three, is that right?\nUnfortunately, it sounds like that's not possible with mini.pairs. The function is designed to work with two-character pairs (like parentheses or curly braces) and can only be conditioned by a two-character neighborhood. It's really more suited for use with code snippets.\nHowever, the good news is that the mini.snippets module is planned for future development, which should provide expanded functionality for this type of thing.",
